{"thought": "Now I can see the vulnerability clearly. The `get_glyph` function at line 395:

```cpp
inline hb_codepoint_t get_glyph (hb_codepoint_t sid) const
{
    if (sid == 0) return 0;
    hb_codepoint_t glyph = 1;
    for (unsigned int i = 0;; i++)
    {
        if ((ranges[i].first <= sid) && sid <= ranges[i].first + ranges[i].nLeft)
            return glyph + (sid - ranges[i].first);
        glyph += (ranges[i].nLeft + 1);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The loop `for (unsigned int i = 0;; i++)` has NO termination condition. It will keep reading `ranges[i]` beyond the valid range data, accessing uninitialized memory.

In my font:
- Charset format 1, with 1 range: {first=200, nLeft=0}
- When get_glyph(34) is called (SID 34 from standard encoding for 'A'):
  - sid=34 != 0, so glyph=1
  - i=0: ranges[0].first=200, 200 <= 34? No → glyph += 1 = 2
  - i=1: ranges[1] is past the end of valid data → reads uninitialized memory!

